### 📘 Big O Notation — A Quick Overview

**Big O Notation** describes the **worst-case time or space complexity** of an algorithm. It tells you **how performance scales** with input size (`n`).

---

### 🚀 Common Big O Time Complexities

| Big O        | Name              | Description                         | Example                          |
| ------------ | ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `O(1)`       | Constant time     | Doesn’t grow with input size        | Accessing an array element       |
| `O(log n)`   | Logarithmic time  | Cuts problem size in half each time | Binary search                    |
| `O(n)`       | Linear time       | Grows directly with input           | Looping through an array         |
| `O(n log n)` | Linearithmic time | Logarithmic growth per element      | Merge sort, quicksort (avg case) |
| `O(n²)`      | Quadratic time    | Double loop over input              | Bubble sort, selection sort      |
| `O(2ⁿ)`      | Exponential time  | Doubles each time `n` increases     | Recursive Fibonacci              |
| `O(n!)`      | Factorial time    | All permutations of input           | Traveling Salesman brute-force   |

---

### 🧠 Why Big O Matters

* **Helps predict scalability**: Will your code work on 10, 1,000, or 1 million elements?
* **Guides algorithm choice**: Faster isn't always obvious—Big O makes it clear.
* **Critical in interviews**: Most coding interviews ask about time and space complexity.

---

### ✨ Tips to Read Big O

* **Drop constants**: `O(2n)` → `O(n)`
* **Drop non-dominant terms**: `O(n² + n)` → `O(n²)`
* Focus on **worst-case** unless stated otherwise.

